--TEST--
Every category of name compiles under exactly one spelling — PascalCase for a class, an interface, an enum, an enum case, a type alias and a namespace segment, camelCase for a method, a property, a parameter and a local, SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE for a class constant and `constructor` for the constructor — only the first character's case is checked so a run of capitals passes anywhere after it, and two names differing in a later character's case are two different names (`rule:core-api/identifier-casing`, `rule:core-api/casing-checks-the-leading-character`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:programs/names-and-casing
namespace App\Billing;

// `rule:core-api/identifier-casing`: each category is checked against exactly
// one pattern, and every name below is the spelling its own category takes.
type OrderId = int;

interface Priced {
    public function priceInCents(): int;
}

enum Rank { Gold = 1, Silver = 2 }

class OrderLine implements Priced {
    public const int MAX_QUANTITY = 10;

    public int $unitPriceInCents = 0;

    public function constructor(int $unitPriceInCents) {
        $this->unitPriceInCents = $unitPriceInCents;
    }

    public function priceInCents(): int {
        return $this->unitPriceInCents * OrderLine::MAX_QUANTITY;
    }
}

OrderId $orderId = 7;
OrderLine $line = new OrderLine(250);
echo $orderId, " ", $line->priceInCents(), "\n";

// `rule:core-api/casing-checks-the-leading-character`: only the first
// character decides, so a run of consecutive capitals is accepted anywhere
// after it, in a class name and inside a method name alike.
class HTTPXMLClient {
    public static function parseXMLPayload(string $payloadBody): uint {
        return Core\Str::length($payloadBody);
    }
}
echo HTTPXMLClient::parseXMLPayload("<order/>"), "\n";

// The same rule leaves two names that differ only in a later character's case
// as two distinct names, in one scope.
int $userId = 1;
int $userID = 2;
echo $userId, $userID, "\n";
--EXPECT--
7 2500
8
12
